<meta name="description" content="One of the most common problems that arises when secondary school students work on problems about area is the confusion between area and perimeter. Looking at a common textbook treatment of area and permiter suggests why this confusion might arise: many textbooks introduce perimeter and area separately, but on consecutive pages of the book, using exactly the same diagrams and examples, often looking at areas of rectangles. A child takes away from this a formula for area (length multiplied by width) and a formula for perimeter ($2l + 2w$) having worked mainly with rectangles." />
